// Where the chained-index rule stops.
//
// `arr[i][j]` is refused (reject/collections/13, 14, 15): both groups walk down
// the same structure, which is what `>` does in one step. A bracket after
// something that is NOT itself an access is one group and not a chain, and this
// file is the record of which is which — the rule was stated in prose first, and
// prose is what a deprecation lived in for five versions.
m = [[1,2,3], [4,5,6], [7,8,9]]

// One group, over a value that was just built or written out. All legal.
>> [1,2,3][2] ¶

f() { <~ [10,20,30] }
>> f()[2] ¶

// An extraction does not descend into `m`: it collects from several paths and
// builds a collection that was nowhere inside it. Indexing that is the same act
// as indexing what `f()` returned.
esquinas = m[[1>1, 1>3] ; [3>1, 3>3]]
>> esquinas ¶
>> esquinas[1] ¶
>> m[[1>1, 1>3] ; [3>1, 3>3]][1] ¶

// The navigator, for contrast: one group, however deep.
>> m[2>3] ¶
>> m[1>1] ¶

// A dot composes with a bracket — different syntax, not a second spelling.
q = #(a: #(b: 7))
>> q["a"].b ¶
>> q.a["b"] ¶
